.PP
Link with \fI-lexpanse\fR.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBexpanse_sync_set_t\fR and \fBexpanse_sync_map_t\fR provide thread-safe,
concurrent digital trie collections.
.PP
Writers employ optimistic lock coupling (OLC) over per-node version
words, allowing concurrent writers on disjoint key expanses to progress
in parallel.
Writers serialize behind an exclusive quiescence lock only for structural
conversions or upon retry exhaustion.
Readers execute an optimistic validation walk using seqlock version
words and Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR).
The protocol is blocking optimistic lock coupling \(em a reader retries
a bounded number of times and then takes the writer mutex \(em not lock-free.
On the common path a reader acquires no mutex and does not block writers.
A reader that exhausts its retries falls back to the writer-excluding path:
it quiesces writers and takes the writer mutex, and writers wait until that
read finishes.
.SH READER HANDLES
For optimal throughput in multithreaded readers, threads should instantiate a
dedicated reader handle (\fBexpanse_sync_map_reader_new\fR) once per thread and
reuse it across queries.
.PP
A reader handle owns a single epoch slot and its pins are not reentrant:
calls on one handle from two threads at once are undefined.
A handle may be passed to, and freed from, a thread other than the one that
created it, once no call on it is in progress; every call releases its epoch
pin before returning, and
.B expanse_sync_map_reader_free
deregisters through the container's registry lock without reading
thread-local state.
Freeing a handle while another thread is inside a call on it, or after its
container has been freed, is undefined.
.PP
The one-shot functions (\fBexpanse_sync_map_get\fR) implicitly register and
deregister epoch participation on each invocation.
.SH ATOMIC READ-MODIFY-WRITE
.B expanse_sync_map_compare_exchange
stores
.I new_value
under
.I key
only if the key's current word is
.IR expected_value ,
as one linearizable step, and may be called from any thread on the shared
handle.
Absence is a flag, never a value, so every
.B uint64_t
is a legal value:
.I expected_present
false means only if the key is absent, and
.I new_present
false removes the key.
It returns true when the store happened.
On both outcomes the word the compare saw is reported through
.I observed_present_out
and
.I observed_out
(either may be NULL;
.I observed_out
is written only when that word was present), so a failed call hands the
caller the word to retry against.
A NULL
.I map
returns false and writes nothing.
.PP
.B expanse_sync_map_get_or_insert
stores
.I value
under
.I key
only if the key is absent, and returns true.
If the key was present it returns false, leaves it untouched and writes its
value through
.I existing_out
(may be NULL).
Of any number of callers racing on one absent key, exactly one gets true.
.PP
There is no callback form.
An update is a loop over
.BR expanse_sync_map_compare_exchange ,
which may run the computation several times, so it must be free of side
effects; the EXAMPLES section gives the loop.
.PP
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_compare_exchange ,
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_get_or_insert ,
.BR expanse_sync_bytesmap_compare_exchange ,
.BR expanse_sync_bytesmap_get_or_insert ,
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_compare_exchange
and
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_get_or_insert
are the same two operations on the string, bytes and ordered-bytes maps,
with the same flags, outputs and return values, each one linearizable
step.
A NULL
.IR map ,
a NULL string key, or a NULL byte-string key with a non-zero
.I len
returns false and writes nothing.
The blob map's forms are in BLOB MAP below.
.SH ORDERED READS
.BR expanse_sync_map_reader_first ,
.BR expanse_sync_map_reader_last ,
.BR expanse_sync_map_reader_next_at_or_after ,
.BR expanse_sync_map_reader_next_after ,
.B expanse_sync_map_reader_prev_at_or_before
and
.B expanse_sync_map_reader_prev_before
return the smallest entry, the largest, and the nearest entry at or after,
after, at or before, and before
.IR key .
Each returns true with the entry written through
.I key_out
and
.I value_out
(either may be NULL), and false when there is no such entry \(em which
includes a successor of
.B UINT64_MAX
and a predecessor of 0 \(em or when
.I reader
is NULL.
.PP
Each is one validated ordered search with the retry budget and fallback of
.BR expanse_sync_map_reader_get :
the entry returned was present, and every key the search passed over was
absent, at one instant.
Walking a range by calling
.B expanse_sync_map_reader_next_after
in a loop answers each step at its own instant, so the sequence is not a
snapshot of the map.
.SH MEMORY ACCOUNTING
.B expanse_sync_map_mem_used
returns the heap bytes used by the map's nodes and leaves, counted as
.BR expanse_map_mem_used (3)
counts them, and 0 when
.I map
is NULL.
It reads with writers excluded: it quiesces writers and takes the writer
mutex, so writers wait for it, and it does not belong in a hot loop.
Nodes retired to the epoch collector but not yet reclaimed are no longer
counted, although their allocations are still resident.
.PP
.B expanse_sync_map_mem_held
and
.B expanse_sync_set_mem_held
return the heap bytes the container holds from the global allocator: the
tree's own share plus what its epoch collector holds for it \(em freed
blocks past their grace period, kept for reuse, and retired blocks still in
their grace period \(em and 0 for a NULL handle.
They read with writers excluded, as
.B expanse_sync_map_mem_used
does.
.PP
.B expanse_sync_map_shrink_to_fit
and
.B expanse_sync_set_shrink_to_fit
return the collector's freed blocks to the global allocator and return the
bytes released (0 for a NULL handle), after which
.B mem_held
is lower by exactly that much.
They run beside readers and writers without excluding either: only blocks
past their grace period are released, and no reader can still hold one.
Blocks still in their grace period stay held until a later call, so
.B mem_held
may still exceed
.B expanse_sync_map_mem_used
afterwards.
.SH STRING MAP
.B expanse_sync_strmap_t
is the concurrent form of
.BR expanse_strmap (3):
an ordered map from NUL-terminated C strings to
.B uint64_t
values.
Its protocol, its container and reader thread rules, and its reader-handle
ownership are those of
.B expanse_sync_map_t
above: free every
.B expanse_sync_strmap_reader_t
before
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_free .
.PP
A key is read up to its terminating NUL, so a key cannot carry an embedded
NUL byte; such keys belong in
.BR expanse_ordered_bytesmap (3).
A NULL
.I key
behaves like a NULL handle: the call returns false (or 0) and writes
nothing.
.PP
The point calls follow the
.B expanse_sync_map_*
conventions.
.B expanse_sync_strmap_insert
returns true when the key is new, and otherwise writes the replaced value
through
.IR old_out .
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_get ,
.B expanse_sync_strmap_remove
and
.B expanse_sync_strmap_reader_get
write through their out-pointer only when they return true.
Every out-pointer may be NULL.
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_mem_used ,
.B expanse_sync_strmap_mem_held
and
.B expanse_sync_strmap_shrink_to_fit
behave as the
.B expanse_sync_map_*
calls of the same name.
.PP
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_reader_first ,
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_reader_last ,
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_reader_next_at_or_after ,
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_reader_next_after ,
.B expanse_sync_strmap_reader_prev_at_or_before
and
.B expanse_sync_strmap_reader_prev_before
are the ordered reads of the string map, with the validation, retry budget
and fallback of
.BR expanse_sync_strmap_reader_get ;
a walk built from
.B expanse_sync_strmap_reader_next_after
is not a snapshot, as for the
.B uint64_t
map.
They copy the key into the caller's buffer under the
.B expanse_strmap_first_ex
contract of
.BR expanse_strmap (3).
.B EXPANSE_STR_NAV_OK
writes the NUL-terminated key to
.IR key_out ,
its length plus one to
.I required_len
and its value to
.IR value_out .
.B EXPANSE_STR_NAV_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
means a key was found but
.I buf_len
is below the length it needs, or
.I key_out
is NULL; only
.I required_len
is written, so NULL with 0 is a length probe.
.B EXPANSE_STR_NAV_NOT_FOUND
means there is no such entry, which includes the predecessor of the empty
key, or that
.I reader
or
.I key
is NULL; nothing is written.
.I required_len
and
.I value_out
may be NULL.
A retry after
.B EXPANSE_STR_NAV_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
is a new read, and may return a different key if a writer ran in between.
.SH BYTES AND ORDERED-BYTES MAPS
.B expanse_sync_bytesmap_t
and
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_t
are the concurrent forms of
.BR expanse_bytesmap (3),
which is unordered, and
.BR expanse_ordered_bytesmap (3):
maps from byte strings to
.B uint64_t
values.
Their protocol, their container and reader thread rules, and their
reader-handle ownership are those of
.B expanse_sync_map_t
above: free every reader before the map.
.PP
A key is the
.I len
bytes at
.IR key :
opaque bytes, with embedded NUL bytes ordinary, and NULL with a
.I len
of 0 the empty key.
A NULL
.I key
with a non-zero
.I len
behaves like a NULL handle: the call returns false (or 0) and writes
nothing.
The point, memory and reader calls follow the
.B expanse_sync_strmap_*
conventions above.
.PP
.BR expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_first ,
.BR expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_last ,
.BR expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_next_at_or_after ,
.BR expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_next_after ,
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_prev_at_or_before
and
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_prev_before
are the ordered reads of the ordered-bytes map.
They take the map handle, not a reader, and they
.B block writers
for their duration: each quiesces writers and takes the writer mutex, so
the entry returned is the answer at one instant and every write waits for
the call.
A walk built from
.B expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_next_after
is not a snapshot: it sees every key present throughout it, in order.
They copy the key into the caller's buffer under the navigation contract
of
.BR expanse_ordered_bytesmap (3).
.B EXPANSE_ORDERED_BYTES_NAV_OK
writes the key to
.IR key_out ,
its length to
.I required_len
and its value to
.IR value_out ;
an empty key needs no buffer.
.B EXPANSE_ORDERED_BYTES_NAV_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
means a key was found but
.I buf_len
is below its length, or
.I key_out
is NULL; only
.I required_len
is written and the buffer is untouched, so NULL with 0 is a length probe.
.B EXPANSE_ORDERED_BYTES_NAV_NOT_FOUND
means there is no such entry, which includes the predecessor of the empty
key, or that
.I map
is NULL, or
.I key
is NULL with a non-zero
.IR len ;
nothing is written.
.I required_len
and
.I value_out
may be NULL, and
.I key
and
.I key_out
may be the same buffer.
A retry after
.B EXPANSE_ORDERED_BYTES_NAV_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
is a new read, and may return a different key if a writer ran in between.
.SH BLOB MAP
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_t
is the concurrent form of
.BR expanse_blob_map (3):
a map from
.B uint64_t
keys to byte payloads with 24-bit hot metadata.
Its protocol, its container and reader thread rules, and its reader-handle
ownership are those of
.B expanse_sync_map_t
above: free every reader before the map.
The reader calls take a non-const handle, because a reader is used by one
thread at a time.
.I chunk_size
and
.I max_capacity
are those of
.BR expanse_blob_map_new_with_capacity ;
0 selects the default.
.PP
Values are copied, never borrowed: no call returns a pointer into the map,
so no later insert, remove or compaction invalidates anything a caller
holds.
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_insert
returns the
.B expanse_blob_status_t
values of
.BR expanse_blob_map_insert_ex .
A value is stored in its slot word, which has no metadata field, only when
.I hot_meta
is 0 (a payload of 7 bytes or fewer, or one the map compresses into the slot
word), and reads back metadata 0.
A payload of any length inserted with non-zero
.I hot_meta
is stored in the arena and keeps it, and
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_META_OVERFLOW
refuses a
.I hot_meta
wider than 24 bits at every length (issue #1548).
.PP
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_get_into
and
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_get_into
copy a payload into the caller's buffer in one read.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_OK
means the payload is in
.IR buf ,
its length in
.I len_out
and its metadata in
.IR meta_out ;
the rest of the buffer is untouched.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_NOT_FOUND
means the key is absent; nothing is written.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
means the key is present and its payload is longer than
.IR buf_len :
nothing is copied, the buffer is untouched, and
.I len_out
and
.I meta_out
receive the payload's length and metadata, from the same read.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_INVALID_ARGUMENT
means a NULL map or reader, a NULL
.I buf
with a non-zero
.IR buf_len ,
or a
.I buf_len
above
.BR PTRDIFF_MAX .
NULL with 0 is the length probe.
.I len_out
and
.I meta_out
may be NULL.
A retry with a buffer of the reported length is a new read: it succeeds
unless a writer replaced or removed the value in between, when it reports
the new state, so a caller loops until
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_OK
or
.BR EXPANSE_BLOB_NOT_FOUND .
.PP
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_get_meta
and
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_contains
and their reader forms return presence; get_meta writes the metadata only
when it returns true.
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_mem_used
and
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_mem_held
exclude writers and wait for a running compaction;
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_mem_used_estimate
takes no lock.
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_compact
returns
.BR EXPANSE_BLOB_OK ,
a refusal, or
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_INVALID_ARGUMENT
for a NULL map; readers keep reading while it copies.
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_set_reclaim_at_cap
is that of
.BR expanse_blob_map (3).
.PP
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_compare_exchange
stores the value
.RI ( new_data ,
.IR new_len ,
.IR new_meta )
under
.I key
only if the key's current value is the expected one, as one linearizable
step; a value is its payload bytes and its metadata, and both are
compared.
.I expected_present
false means only if the key is absent, and
.I new_present
false removes the key; the data, length and metadata beside a false flag
are ignored.
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_get_or_insert
stores
.RI ( data ,
.IR len ,
.IR hot_meta )
only if the key is absent.
Both return
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_OK
when they stored, and write nothing then.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_MISMATCH
means nothing was stored: the current value was not the expected one
(compare_exchange writes its presence to
.IR observed_present_out ),
or the key was present (get_or_insert).
A present value's payload is copied to
.I buf
with its length in
.I len_out
and its metadata in
.IR meta_out ;
an absent one writes 0 to both.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL
is a mismatch whose payload is longer than
.IR buf_len :
nothing is copied, and the length and metadata are written from the same
step that compared.
It never accompanies an insert: get_or_insert writes the buffer only when
the key was present.
A refusal
.RB ( EXPANSE_BLOB_META_OVERFLOW ,
.BR EXPANSE_BLOB_ALLOCATION_FAILED ,
.BR EXPANSE_BLOB_CAP_REFUSED ,
.BR EXPANSE_BLOB_ARENA_FULL )
means the comparison held (get_or_insert: the key was absent) but the new
value cannot be stored; the map is unchanged and nothing is written.
These calls do not run the reclaim at the capacity cap that
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_insert
runs.
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_INVALID_ARGUMENT
means a NULL map, a present value with NULL data and a non-zero length, a
length above
.BR PTRDIFF_MAX ,
or a NULL
.I buf
with a non-zero
.IR buf_len .
.I buf
may be the buffer passed as
.I expected_data
or
.IR data ,
so a retry loop can reuse one buffer: it retries on a mismatch and stops
on
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_OK
or a refusal, which is reported only when the comparison held.
.PP
.B expanse_sync_blob_map_compare_exchange_meta
swaps the 24-bit metadata in the slot, with no payload copy and no
allocation, only if the current metadata is
.IR expected_meta ;
a payload stored in its slot word has metadata 0.
It returns
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_OK
when it swapped (or, with
.I new_present
false, removed),
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_MISMATCH
with the presence and metadata it saw,
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_META_OVERFLOW
for a
.I new_meta
wider than 24 bits, or
.B EXPANSE_BLOB_NO_META_FIELD
when the comparison held but the value has no metadata field: the key is
absent, or
.I new_meta
is non-zero for a payload stored in its slot word.
.PP
A value reads back the metadata it was inserted with at every payload
length, so expecting that metadata matches (issue #1548).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <inttypes.h>
#include <expanse.h>

int main(void) {
    expanse_sync_map_t *sync_map = expanse_sync_map_new();

    /* Writer inserts keys */
    expanse_sync_map_insert(sync_map, 42, 1000, NULL);

    /* Reader thread acquires a handle */
    expanse_sync_map_reader_t *reader =
        expanse_sync_map_reader_new(sync_map);

    uint64_t val;
    if (expanse_sync_map_reader_get(reader, 42, &val)) {
        printf("Concurrent read: 42 -> %" PRIu64 "\\n", val);
    }

    expanse_sync_map_reader_free(reader);

    /* update(42, v + 1) as a loop over compare_exchange */
    uint64_t cur = val;
    bool present = true;
    for (;;) {
        bool seen_present;
        uint64_t seen;
        if (expanse_sync_map_compare_exchange(sync_map, 42,
                present, cur, true, cur + 1, &seen_present, &seen))
            break;
        present = seen_present;
        cur = seen;
    }
    expanse_sync_map_get(sync_map, 42, &val);
    printf("After update: 42 -> %" PRIu64 "\\n", val);

    expanse_sync_map_free(sync_map);

    /* The shared string map, read through a reader handle */
    expanse_sync_strmap_t *names = expanse_sync_strmap_new();
    expanse_sync_strmap_insert(names, "alpha", 1, NULL);
    expanse_sync_strmap_insert(names, "beta", 2, NULL);

    expanse_sync_strmap_reader_t *sr =
        expanse_sync_strmap_reader_new(names);
    if (expanse_sync_strmap_reader_get(sr, "beta", &val)) {
        printf("String read: beta -> %" PRIu64 "\\n", val);
    }

    /* Ordered walk: a NULL, 0 probe reports the length a key needs */
    size_t need = 0;
    expanse_sync_strmap_reader_first(sr, NULL, 0, &need, NULL);
    printf("First key needs %zu bytes\\n", need);
    char key[16], prev[16];
    expanse_str_nav_status st =
        expanse_sync_strmap_reader_first(sr, key, sizeof key, &need, &val);
    while (st == EXPANSE_STR_NAV_OK) {
        printf("Walk: %s -> %" PRIu64 "\\n", key, val);
        memcpy(prev, key, need);
        st = expanse_sync_strmap_reader_next_after(sr, prev, key,
                                                   sizeof key, &need, &val);
    }
    expanse_sync_strmap_reader_free(sr);

    /* update("alpha", v * 10) over compare_exchange */
    present = expanse_sync_strmap_get(names, "alpha", &cur);
    for (;;) {
        bool seen_present;
        uint64_t seen;
        if (expanse_sync_strmap_compare_exchange(names, "alpha",
                present, cur, true, cur * 10, &seen_present, &seen))
            break;
        present = seen_present;
        cur = seen;
    }
    if (!expanse_sync_strmap_get_or_insert(names, "beta", 9, &val)) {
        printf("String get_or_insert: beta stays %" PRIu64 "\n", val);
    }
    expanse_sync_strmap_get(names, "alpha", &val);
    printf("String update: alpha -> %" PRIu64 "\n", val);

    expanse_sync_strmap_free(names);

    /* The shared ordered-bytes map: keys carry embedded NULs */
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_t *ob = expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_new();
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_insert(ob, "k\\0b", 3, 20, NULL);
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_insert(ob, "k\\0a", 3, 10, NULL);

    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_reader_t *obr =
        expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_reader_new(ob);
    if (expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_reader_get(obr, "k\\0b", 3, &val)) {
        printf("Bytes read: 3-byte key -> %" PRIu64 "\\n", val);
    }
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_reader_free(obr);

    /* Ordered reads take the map handle and block writers per call */
    unsigned char bk[8];
    expanse_ordered_bytes_nav_status bs =
        expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_first(ob, bk, sizeof bk, &need, &val);
    while (bs == EXPANSE_ORDERED_BYTES_NAV_OK) {
        printf("Bytes walk: %zu bytes, last 0x%02x -> %" PRIu64 "\\n",
               need, bk[need - 1], val);
        bs = expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_next_after(ob, bk, need, bk,
                                                      sizeof bk, &need, &val);
    }
    /* The byte-string maps take the same loop, with a (key, len) key */
    present = expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_get(ob, "k\0a", 3, &cur);
    for (;;) {
        bool seen_present;
        uint64_t seen;
        if (expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_compare_exchange(ob, "k\0a", 3,
                present, cur, true, cur + 5, &seen_present, &seen))
            break;
        present = seen_present;
        cur = seen;
    }
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_get(ob, "k\0a", 3, &val);
    printf("Bytes update: 3-byte key -> %" PRIu64 "\n", val);
    expanse_sync_ordered_bytesmap_free(ob);

    /* The shared blob map: a payload copied out through a caller buffer */
    expanse_sync_blob_map_t *blobs = expanse_sync_blob_map_new(0);
    const char *doc = "a payload longer than the first buffer";
    expanse_sync_blob_map_insert(blobs, 7, (const uint8_t *) doc,
                                 strlen(doc), 3);
    expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_t *br =
        expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_new(blobs);
    uint8_t small[8], big[64];
    size_t len = 0;
    uint32_t meta = 0;
    expanse_blob_status_t rc = expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_get_into(
        br, 7, small, sizeof small, &len, &meta);
    if (rc == EXPANSE_BLOB_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL) {
        printf("Blob needs %zu bytes, metadata %u\\n", len, meta);
        /* A retry is a new read; loop until OK or NOT_FOUND */
        uint8_t *dst = len <= sizeof big ? big : NULL;
        rc = expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_get_into(br, 7, dst, len,
                                                   &len, &meta);
    }
    if (rc == EXPANSE_BLOB_OK) {
        printf("Blob read: %.*s\\n", (int) len, (const char *) big);
    }
    expanse_sync_blob_map_reader_free(br);

    /* get_or_insert on a present key reports its value; retry when the
     * buffer is too small. Nothing was stored either way. */
    rc = expanse_sync_blob_map_get_or_insert(blobs, 7,
        (const uint8_t *) "other", 5, 0, small, sizeof small, &len, &meta);
    if (rc == EXPANSE_BLOB_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL && len <= sizeof big) {
        rc = expanse_sync_blob_map_get_or_insert(blobs, 7,
            (const uint8_t *) "other", 5, 0, big, sizeof big, &len, &meta);
    }
    if (rc == EXPANSE_BLOB_MISMATCH) {
        printf("Blob kept: %zu bytes, metadata %u\n", len, meta);
    }

    /* A version counter in the metadata: compare_exchange_meta, no copy */
    bool present7 = expanse_sync_blob_map_get_meta(blobs, 7, &meta);
    for (;;) {
        bool seen_present;
        uint32_t seen;
        rc = expanse_sync_blob_map_compare_exchange_meta(blobs, 7, present7,
            meta, true, meta + 1, &seen_present, &seen);
        if (rc != EXPANSE_BLOB_MISMATCH)
            break; /* OK, or a refusal: stop */
        present7 = seen_present;
        meta = seen;
    }
    expanse_sync_blob_map_get_meta(blobs, 7, &meta);
    printf("Blob version: %u\n", meta);
    expanse_sync_blob_map_free(blobs);
    return 0;
}
.fi
